1
00:00:03,359 --> 00:00:08,558
The felicitous idea occurred to me a morning or two later when I woke, that the best step I could

2
00:00:08,559 --> 00:00:15,159
take towards making myself uncommon was to get out of Biddy everything she knew. In pursuance of this

3
00:00:15,159 --> 00:00:20,558
luminous conception I mentioned to Biddy when I went to Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s at night, that

4
00:00:20,559 --> 00:00:25,680
I had a particular reason for wishing to get on in life, and that I should feel very much obliged

5
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:32,398
to her if she would impart all her learning to me. Biddy, who was the most obliging of girls,

6
00:00:32,398 --> 00:00:39,439
immediately said she would, and indeed began to carry out her promise within five minutes.

7
00:00:39,439 --> 00:00:45,239
The Educational scheme or Course established by Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt may be resolved into

8
00:00:45,239 --> 00:00:53,239
the following synopsis. The pupils ate apples and put straws down one another’s backs, until

9
00:00:53,239 --> 00:00:59,040
Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt collected her energies, and made an indiscriminate totter at them with a

10
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:06,200
birch-rod. After receiving the charge with every mark of derision, the pupils formed in line and

11
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:13,279
buzzingly passed a ragged book from hand to hand. The book had an alphabet in it, some figures and

12
00:01:13,279 --> 00:01:20,519
tables, and a little spelling,—that is to say, it had had once. As soon as this volume began

13
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:26,680
to circulate, Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt fell into a state of coma, arising either from sleep or a

14
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:34,359
rheumatic paroxysm. The pupils then entered among themselves upon a competitive examination on the

15
00:01:34,359 --> 00:01:40,879
subject of Boots, with the view of ascertaining who could tread the hardest upon whose toes. This

16
00:01:40,879 --> 00:01:47,639
mental exercise lasted until Biddy made a rush at them and distributed three defaced Bibles (shaped

17
00:01:47,639 --> 00:01:53,239
as if they had been unskilfully cut off the chump end of something), more illegibly printed at the

18
00:01:53,239 --> 00:01:59,318
best than any curiosities of literature I have since met with, speckled all over with ironmould,

19
00:01:59,318 --> 00:02:04,679
and having various specimens of the insect world smashed between their leaves.

20
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:09,639
This part of the Course was usually lightened by several single combats between Biddy and

21
00:02:09,639 --> 00:02:16,040
refractory students. When the fights were over, Biddy gave out the number of a page,

22
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:21,318
and then we all read aloud what we could,—or what we couldn’t—in a frightful chorus;

23
00:02:21,318 --> 00:02:27,478
Biddy leading with a high, shrill, monotonous voice, and none of us having the least notion of,

24
00:02:27,478 --> 00:02:34,398
or reverence for, what we were reading about. When this horrible din had lasted a certain time,

25
00:02:34,398 --> 00:02:40,478
it mechanically awoke Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt, who staggered at a boy fortuitously, and pulled

26
00:02:40,478 --> 00:02:46,558
his ears. This was understood to terminate the Course for the evening, and we emerged into the

27
00:02:46,559 --> 00:02:53,239
air with shrieks of intellectual victory. It is fair to remark that there was no prohibition

28
00:02:53,239 --> 00:02:59,120
against any pupil’s entertaining himself with a slate or even with the ink (when there was any),

29
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:03,760
but that it was not easy to pursue that branch of study in the winter season,

30
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:08,600
on account of the little general shop in which the classes were holden—and which

31
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:13,318
was also Mr. Wopsle’s great-aunt’s sitting-room and bedchamber—being

32
00:03:13,318 --> 00:03:20,718
but faintly illuminated through the agency of one low-spirited dip-candle and no snuffers.

33
00:03:20,719 --> 00:03:25,840
It appeared to me that it would take time to become uncommon, under these circumstances:

34
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:32,398
nevertheless, I resolved to try it, and that very evening Biddy entered on our special agreement,

35
00:03:32,398 --> 00:03:36,239
by imparting some information from her little catalogue of Prices,

36
00:03:36,239 --> 00:03:41,079
under the head of moist sugar, and lending me, to copy at home,

37
00:03:41,079 --> 00:03:46,279
a large old English D which she had imitated from the heading of some newspaper,

38
00:03:46,279 --> 00:03:52,318
and which I supposed, until she told me what it was, to be a design for a buckle.

39
00:03:52,318 --> 00:03:54,799
Of course there was a public-house in the village,

40
00:03:54,799 --> 00:04:00,559
and of course Joe liked sometimes to smoke his pipe there. I had received strict orders from

41
00:04:00,559 --> 00:04:05,879
my sister to call for him at the Three Jolly Bargemen, that evening, on my way from school,

42
00:04:05,879 --> 00:04:13,478
and bring him home at my peril. To the Three Jolly Bargemen, therefore, I directed my steps.

43
00:04:13,478 --> 00:04:18,318
There was a bar at the Jolly Bargemen, with some alarmingly long chalk scores in it on the wall

44
00:04:18,318 --> 00:04:24,679
at the side of the door, which seemed to me to be never paid off. They had been there ever since I

45
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:31,439
could remember, and had grown more than I had. But there was a quantity of chalk about our country,

46
00:04:31,439 --> 00:04:36,200
and perhaps the people neglected no opportunity of turning it to account.

47
00:04:36,200 --> 00:04:41,679
It being Saturday night, I found the landlord looking rather grimly at these records; but as my

48
00:04:41,680 --> 00:04:47,159
business was with Joe and not with him, I merely wished him good evening, and passed into the

49
00:04:47,159 --> 00:04:53,359
common room at the end of the passage, where there was a bright large kitchen fire, and where Joe was

50
00:04:53,359 --> 00:05:00,239
smoking his pipe in company with Mr. Wopsle and a stranger. Joe greeted me as usual with “Halloa,

51
00:05:00,239 --> 00:05:06,359
Pip, old chap!” and the moment he said that, the stranger turned his head and looked at me.

52
00:05:06,359 --> 00:05:12,920
He was a secret-looking man whom I had never seen before. His head was all on one side,

53
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:17,239
and one of his eyes was half shut up, as if he were taking aim at something with

54
00:05:17,239 --> 00:05:23,959
an invisible gun. He had a pipe in his mouth, and he took it out, and, after slowly blowing

55
00:05:23,959 --> 00:05:29,959
all his smoke away and looking hard at me all the time, nodded. So, I nodded,

56
00:05:29,959 --> 00:05:36,478
and then he nodded again, and made room on the settle beside him that I might sit down there.

57
00:05:36,478 --> 00:05:41,279
But as I was used to sit beside Joe whenever I entered that place of resort, I said “No,

58
00:05:41,279 --> 00:05:48,599
thank you, sir,” and fell into the space Joe made for me on the opposite settle. The strange man,

59
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:53,600
after glancing at Joe, and seeing that his attention was otherwise engaged, nodded to me

60
00:05:53,600 --> 00:06:00,600
again when I had taken my seat, and then rubbed his leg—in a very odd way, as it struck me.

61
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:06,318
“You was saying,” said the strange man, turning to Joe, “that you was a blacksmith.”

62
00:06:06,318 --> 00:06:09,599
“Yes. I said it, you know,” said Joe.

63
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:14,239
“What’ll you drink, Mr.—? You didn’t mention your name, by the bye.”

64
00:06:14,239 --> 00:06:19,079
Joe mentioned it now, and the strange man called him by it. “What’ll you drink,

65
00:06:19,079 --> 00:06:22,760
Mr. Gargery? At my expense? To top up with?”

66
00:06:22,760 --> 00:06:25,520
“Well,” said Joe, “to tell you the truth,

67
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:29,279
I ain’t much in the habit of drinking at anybody’s expense but my own.”

68
00:06:29,279 --> 00:06:34,079
“Habit? No,” returned the stranger, “but once and away, and on a Saturday

69
00:06:34,079 --> 00:06:38,478
night too. Come! Put a name to it, Mr. Gargery.”

70
00:06:38,478 --> 00:06:42,478
“I wouldn’t wish to be stiff company,” said Joe. “Rum.”

71
00:06:42,478 --> 00:06:47,239
“Rum,” repeated the stranger. “And will the other gentleman originate a sentiment.”

72
00:06:47,239 --> 00:06:49,398
“Rum,” said Mr. Wopsle.

73
00:06:49,398 --> 00:06:54,439
“Three Rums!” cried the stranger, calling to the landlord. “Glasses round!”

74
00:06:54,439 --> 00:06:59,040
“This other gentleman,” observed Joe, by way of introducing Mr. Wopsle,

75
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:03,840
“is a gentleman that you would like to hear give it out. Our clerk at church.”

76
00:07:04,398 --> 00:07:09,239
“Aha!” said the stranger, quickly, and cocking his eye at me. “The lonely church,

77
00:07:09,239 --> 00:07:12,638
right out on the marshes, with graves round it!”

78
00:07:12,639 --> 00:07:14,200
“That’s it,” said Joe.

79
00:07:14,200 --> 00:07:19,120
The stranger, with a comfortable kind of grunt over his pipe, put his legs up on the settle

80
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:25,318
that he had to himself. He wore a flapping broad-brimmed traveller’s hat, and under it

81
00:07:25,318 --> 00:07:32,920
a handkerchief tied over his head in the manner of a cap: so that he showed no hair. As he looked at

82
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:40,959
the fire, I thought I saw a cunning expression, followed by a half-laugh, come into his face.

83
00:07:40,959 --> 00:07:43,639
“I am not acquainted with this country, gentlemen,

84
00:07:43,639 --> 00:07:47,680
but it seems a solitary country towards the river.”

85
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:50,920
“Most marshes is solitary,” said Joe.

86
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:57,959
“No doubt, no doubt. Do you find any gypsies, now, or tramps, or vagrants of any sort, out there?”

87
00:07:57,959 --> 00:08:00,920
“No,” said Joe; “none but a runaway convict now

88
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:05,639
and then. And we don’t find them, easy. Eh, Mr. Wopsle?”

89
00:08:05,639 --> 00:08:12,600
Mr. Wopsle, with a majestic remembrance of old discomfiture, assented; but not warmly.

90
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:16,879
“Seems you have been out after such?” asked the stranger.

91
00:08:16,879 --> 00:08:22,040
“Once,” returned Joe. “Not that we wanted to take them, you understand;

92
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:27,760
we went out as lookers on; me, and Mr. Wopsle, and Pip. Didn’t us, Pip?”

93
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:29,200
“Yes, Joe.”

94
00:08:29,200 --> 00:08:35,040
The stranger looked at me again,—still cocking his eye, as if he were expressly taking aim at me with

95
00:08:35,040 --> 00:08:42,439
his invisible gun,—and said, “He’s a likely young parcel of bones that. What is it you call him?”

96
00:08:42,439 --> 00:08:43,959
“Pip,” said Joe.

97
00:08:43,959 --> 00:08:45,439
“Christened Pip?”

98
00:08:45,439 --> 00:08:47,679
“No, not christened Pip.”

99
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:49,359
“Surname Pip?”

100
00:08:49,359 --> 00:08:55,478
“No,” said Joe, “it’s a kind of family name what he gave himself when a infant, and is called by.”

101
00:08:55,478 --> 00:08:57,519
“Son of yours?”

102
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:01,600
“Well,” said Joe, meditatively, not, of course, that it could be in anywise

103
00:09:01,600 --> 00:09:05,680
necessary to consider about it, but because it was the way at the Jolly Bargemen to seem

104
00:09:05,680 --> 00:09:13,478
to consider deeply about everything that was discussed over pipes,—“well—no. No, he ain’t.”

105
00:09:13,478 --> 00:09:16,159
“Nevvy?” said the strange man.

106
00:09:16,159 --> 00:09:20,398
“Well,” said Joe, with the same appearance of profound cogitation,

107
00:09:20,398 --> 00:09:25,759
“he is not—no, not to deceive you, he is not—my nevvy.”

108
00:09:25,760 --> 00:09:29,000
“What the Blue Blazes is he?” asked the stranger.

109
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,639
Which appeared to me to be an inquiry of unnecessary strength.

110
00:09:32,639 --> 00:09:37,680
Mr. Wopsle struck in upon that; as one who knew all about relationships,

111
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:42,600
having professional occasion to bear in mind what female relations a man might not marry;

112
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:48,200
and expounded the ties between me and Joe. Having his hand in,

113
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:53,398
Mr. Wopsle finished off with a most terrifically snarling passage from Richard the Third,

114
00:09:53,398 --> 00:10:00,120
and seemed to think he had done quite enough to account for it when he added, “—as the poet says.”

115
00:10:00,120 --> 00:10:06,120
And here I may remark that when Mr. Wopsle referred to me, he considered it a necessary

116
00:10:06,120 --> 00:10:12,439
part of such reference to rumple my hair and poke it into my eyes. I cannot conceive why everybody

117
00:10:12,439 --> 00:10:17,439
of his standing who visited at our house should always have put me through the same inflammatory

118
00:10:17,439 --> 00:10:23,519
process under similar circumstances. Yet I do not call to mind that I was ever in my earlier

119
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:29,719
youth the subject of remark in our social family circle, but some large-handed person

120
00:10:29,719 --> 00:10:35,879
took some such ophthalmic steps to patronise me. All this while, the strange man looked at nobody

121
00:10:35,879 --> 00:10:42,680
but me, and looked at me as if he were determined to have a shot at me at last, and bring me down.

122
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:47,520
But he said nothing after offering his Blue Blazes observation, until the glasses of rum

123
00:10:47,520 --> 00:10:53,879
and water were brought; and then he made his shot, and a most extraordinary shot it was.

124
00:10:53,879 --> 00:10:59,200
It was not a verbal remark, but a proceeding in dumb-show, and was pointedly addressed to

125
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:05,519
me. He stirred his rum and water pointedly at me, and he tasted his rum and water pointedly at me.

126
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:11,799
And he stirred it and he tasted it; not with a spoon that was brought to him, but with a file.

127
00:11:11,799 --> 00:11:18,478
He did this so that nobody but I saw the file; and when he had done it he wiped the file and put it

128
00:11:18,478 --> 00:11:24,718
in a breast-pocket. I knew it to be Joe’s file, and I knew that he knew my convict,

129
00:11:24,719 --> 00:11:31,639
the moment I saw the instrument. I sat gazing at him, spell-bound. But he now reclined on

130
00:11:31,639 --> 00:11:38,159
his settle, taking very little notice of me, and talking principally about turnips.

131
00:11:38,159 --> 00:11:42,719
There was a delicious sense of cleaning-up and making a quiet pause before going on in

132
00:11:42,719 --> 00:11:47,719
life afresh, in our village on Saturday nights, which stimulated Joe to dare to

133
00:11:47,719 --> 00:11:52,680
stay out half an hour longer on Saturdays than at other times. The half-hour and the

134
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:58,840
rum and water running out together, Joe got up to go, and took me by the hand.

135
00:11:58,840 --> 00:12:04,040
“Stop half a moment, Mr. Gargery,” said the strange man. “I think I’ve got a bright new

136
00:12:04,040 --> 00:12:08,760
shilling somewhere in my pocket, and if I have, the boy shall have it.”

137
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:13,398
He looked it out from a handful of small change, folded it in some crumpled paper,

138
00:12:13,398 --> 00:12:19,120
and gave it to me. “Yours!” said he. “Mind! Your own.”

139
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:24,200
I thanked him, staring at him far beyond the bounds of good manners, and holding tight to

140
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:30,398
Joe. He gave Joe good-night, and he gave Mr. Wopsle good-night (who went out with us),

141
00:12:30,398 --> 00:12:33,920
and he gave me only a look with his aiming eye,—no, not a look,

142
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:39,478
for he shut it up, but wonders may be done with an eye by hiding it.

143
00:12:39,478 --> 00:12:44,879
On the way home, if I had been in a humour for talking, the talk must have been all on my side,

144
00:12:44,879 --> 00:12:50,040
for Mr. Wopsle parted from us at the door of the Jolly Bargemen, and Joe went all the way home with

145
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:56,000
his mouth wide open, to rinse the rum out with as much air as possible. But I was in a manner

146
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:03,000
stupefied by this turning up of my old misdeed and old acquaintance, and could think of nothing else.

147
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:07,478
My sister was not in a very bad temper when we presented ourselves in the kitchen,

148
00:13:07,478 --> 00:13:11,200
and Joe was encouraged by that unusual circumstance to tell her about the

149
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:16,478
bright shilling. “A bad un, I’ll be bound,” said Mrs. Joe triumphantly,

150
00:13:16,478 --> 00:13:20,120
“or he wouldn’t have given it to the boy! Let’s look at it.”

151
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:26,120
I took it out of the paper, and it proved to be a good one. “But what’s this?” said Mrs. Joe,

152
00:13:26,120 --> 00:13:31,079
throwing down the shilling and catching up the paper. “Two One-Pound notes?”

153
00:13:31,079 --> 00:13:36,398
Nothing less than two fat sweltering one-pound notes that seemed to have been on terms of the

154
00:13:36,398 --> 00:13:43,279
warmest intimacy with all the cattle-markets in the county. Joe caught up his hat again,

155
00:13:43,279 --> 00:13:49,079
and ran with them to the Jolly Bargemen to restore them to their owner. While he was gone,

156
00:13:49,079 --> 00:13:53,680
I sat down on my usual stool and looked vacantly at my sister,

157
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:57,760
feeling pretty sure that the man would not be there.

158
00:13:57,760 --> 00:14:04,238
Presently, Joe came back, saying that the man was gone, but that he, Joe, had left word at the

159
00:14:04,238 --> 00:14:11,120
Three Jolly Bargemen concerning the notes. Then my sister sealed them up in a piece of paper,

160
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:15,318
and put them under some dried rose-leaves in an ornamental teapot on the top of a press

161
00:14:15,318 --> 00:14:24,558
in the state parlour. There they remained, a nightmare to me, many and many a night and day.

162
00:14:24,559 --> 00:14:29,238
I had sadly broken sleep when I got to bed, through thinking of the strange man taking

163
00:14:29,238 --> 00:14:34,840
aim at me with his invisible gun, and of the guiltily coarse and common thing it was, to be

164
00:14:34,840 --> 00:14:41,359
on secret terms of conspiracy with convicts,—a feature in my low career that I had previously

165
00:14:41,359 --> 00:14:48,519
forgotten. I was haunted by the file too. A dread possessed me that when I least expected it,

166
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:55,238
the file would reappear. I coaxed myself to sleep by thinking of Miss Havisham’s, next Wednesday;

167
00:14:55,238 --> 00:14:59,638
and in my sleep I saw the file coming at me out of a door, without seeing who held it,

168
00:14:59,639 --> 00:15:03,000
and I screamed myself awake. Chapter 11.
